readings.c3s1:
¶
{{smaller block|I have pursued mine enemies, and destroyed them; and turned not ''again'' till I had consumed them.
{{right|2 Sam. xxii. 38.}}}}

'''2.''' Of action: Back; in return; in reply, response, answer, echo.
{{smaller block|Do good, and lend, hoping for nothing ''again.''
{{right|Luke vi. 35.}}
Who art thou that answerest ''again?'' {{right|Rom. ix. 20.}}
All Israel shouted with a great shout, so that the earth rang ''again.'' {{right|1 Sam. iv. 5.}}
<poem>I knit my hand-kercher about your brows; . . .
And I did never ask it you ''again.'' ''Shak.'', K. John, iv. 1.</poem>
He laughed till the glasses on the sideboard rang ''again.''
{{right|''Dickens'', Pickwick, I. 261.}}}}

'''3.''' Of action or fact as related to time, or of time simply: Once more; in addition; another time; anew: marking repetition—(''a'') Of action or existence: as, to do anything ''again;'' he had to make it all over ''again.''
{{smaller block|I will not ''again'' curse the ground any more, . . . neither will I ''again'' smite any more every thing living, as I have done. {{right|Gen. viii. 21.}}
If a man die, shall he live ''again?'' {{right|Job xiv. 14.}}
<poem>Quicken the Past to life ''again.''</poem>
{{right|''Whittier'', The Norseman.}}}}
(''b'') Of number or quantity: only in the phrases ''as much'' or ''as many again'' (=twice as much or as many), ''half as much again'' (= once and a half as much), etc. (''c'') Of kind or character: marking resemblance.
{{smaller block|There is not in the world ''again'' such a spring and seminary of brave military people as in England, Scotland, and Ireland. {{right|''Bacon.''}}}}

'''4.''' Of succession of thought: Once more; in continuation; in an additional case or instance;

readings.c3s2:
moreover; besides (marking transition); on the other hand (marking contrast).

{{smaller block|<poem>''Again'', there is sprung up
An heretic, an arch one, Cranmer.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hen. VIII., iii. 2.}}}}

{{smaller block|He was sometimes sad, and sometimes ''again'' profusely merry.
{{right|''Burton'', Anat. of Mel., p. 49.}}}}

'''Again and again''', often; with frequent repetition.

{{smaller block|Good books should be read ''again and again'', and thought about, talked about, considered and re-considered.
{{right|''J. F. Clarke'', Self-Culture, p. 323.}}}}

'''Now and again''', now and then; occasionally.—'''Once and again''', repeatedly.

{{smaller block|The effects of which he had ''once and again'' experienced.
{{right|''Brougham.''}}}}

'''To and again''', to and fro; backward and forward.
{{smaller|[The adverb ''again'' was much used in Middle English, and less frequently in Anglo-Saxon, in loose composition with verbs or verbal derivatives, as equivalent to, and generally as an express translation of, the Latin prefix ''re-'', as in ''again-fight'' (L. ''re-pugnare''), ''again-rising'' (L. ''re-surrectio''), ''again-buy'' (L. ''red-imere''), ''again-stand'' (L. ''re-sistere''); or of Latin ''contra-'', as ''again-say'' (L. ''contra-dicere''), etc.; being in this use variable with ''gain-'', q. v. Only a few such compounds are entered below.]}}

'''II.†''' ''prep.'' Against.

{{smaller block|''Ageyn'' another hethen in Turkye.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Gen. Prol. to C. T., l. 66.}}}}

{{smaller block|[''Again'', prep., was formerly in use in all the senses of ''against'' by which in literary use it has been displaced. It is still common in dialectal speech, pronounced agen or agin: as, I have nothing ''agin'' him.]}}

'''III.†''' ''conj.'' Against the time that: like ''against'', conj. [In this use now only dialectal.]

{{smaller block|<poem>Bid your fellows
Get all their flails ready ''again'' I come.</poem>
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Every Man out of his Humour, i. 1.}}}}
